<p>Few things are as frustrating as a denial letter when you can see the damage with your own eyes. If you are dealing with a <strong>denied insurance claim in San Antonio</strong>, the most useful step is to slow down and work out exactly why the insurer said no. Some denials rest on a real exclusion in the policy. Others rest on an incomplete inspection, missing documentation, or a cause-of-loss opinion that can be tested with better evidence. San Antonio Public Adjuster helps homeowners and business owners across Bexar County review denials and decide on a sensible next step.</p>

<h2>Claims That Commonly Get Denied in San Antonio</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hail and roof claims</strong> where the insurer says the damage is cosmetic, pre-existing, or caused by age. See <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Wind claims</strong> where lifted or creased shingles are blamed on installation or deterioration.</li>
<li><strong>Interior water claims</strong> where the insurer finds no storm-created opening or calls the leak long-term seepage.</li>
<li><strong>Freeze and plumbing claims</strong> where the insurer questions whether the home was heated or the water supply was shut off. See <a href="/services/freeze-damage-claims/">freeze damage claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Flood-related losses</strong> under homeowners or commercial policies that exclude flood. See <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Foundation and cracking claims</strong> where the insurer attributes movement to expansive clay soils rather than a covered event.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why Denials Happen After Bexar County Storms</h2>
<p>Bexar County's hail season peaks in April and May, and large storms can produce a flood of claims within days. NOAA has logged more than 600 hail reports in the county since 1957, and events like the April 12, 2016 storm across northwest San Antonio produced hail up to 4.5 inches near Tezel Road and Bandera Road. When insurers bring in outside adjusters to handle that volume, inspections can be brief, and some damage, especially on steep roofs, metal accessories, stucco, or limestone, is easy to overlook. Older composition roofs also invite wear-and-tear arguments even when fresh storm damage is present.</p>
<p>Weather adds another wrinkle. San Antonio sits in "Flash Flood Alley," so heavy rain events can blur the line between covered wind-driven rain and excluded flood. None of this means a denial is wrong, but it does mean a careful second look is often worthwhile.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/What-to-Do-If-Your-Hail-Claim-Was-Denied-in-Texas-3-1024x683.webp" alt="Two inspectors on a roof examining hail damage to shingles" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>A second, careful roof inspection can show whether damage the insurer called wear and tear is consistent with hail.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>How to Read Your Denial Letter</h2>
<p>A denial letter should identify the policy provisions the insurer is relying on. Read it side by side with your full policy, not on its own. Look for:</p>
<ul>
<li>The specific exclusion, condition, or definition quoted</li>
<li>Whether the denial is total or partial (for example, roof approved but interior denied)</li>
<li>The inspection date and who inspected: a field adjuster, ladder assist, or engineer</li>
<li>Any reference to a report, photos, or weather data</li>
<li>Any deadline for providing additional information</li>
</ul>
<p>If the letter is vague, ask the insurer in writing to identify the exact policy language and facts it relied on. A clear explanation makes it much easier to decide what to do next.</p>

<h2>Common Denial Reasons and Evidence That May Help</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Denial reason</th><th>What the insurer is saying</th><th>Evidence that may be relevant</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Wear and tear / deterioration</td><td>Damage came from age, not a sudden event</td><td>Roof age, pre-loss photos, close-ups of fresh fractures or bruising</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pre-existing damage</td><td>Damage happened before the policy period or reported date</td><td>NOAA records for the date of loss, prior inspection reports, home purchase inspection</td></tr>
<tr><td>Late notice</td><td>Delay in reporting hurt the insurer's ability to investigate</td><td>When damage was discovered, communication records, reasons for the delay</td></tr>
<tr><td>Excluded peril</td><td>Cause falls under an exclusion (flood, mold, seepage, settling)</td><td>Cause-and-origin evidence, plumber, contractor, or engineer reports</td></tr>
<tr><td>No storm-created opening</td><td>Rain entered without wind or hail first damaging the building</td><td>Roof and wall photos at the leak location, matching interior staining</td></tr>
<tr><td>Below deductible</td><td>Covered damage is less than the deductible</td><td>A full, independent line-item estimate</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Weather records can help tie damage to a specific date. Our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup</a> and <a href="/storm-history/">San Antonio storm history</a> pages draw on NOAA data for Bexar County.</p>

